  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe ongoing passage of a cold front will carry in a southwest-to \nnortheast oriented band of showers ahead and along the front through\nthe afternoon and evening hours. The frontal boundary will exit east\nof the cwa between 01-02Z which will mark the end of precipitation \nchances. Passage of this boundary will also bring an end to the \nrecent stretch of well above normal temperatures as northwest flow \nand cold air advection become established. Temperatures tomorrow \nwill cap in the low 40s for a high. A secondary push of colder air \nwill arrive late tomorrow as a deepening mid-level wave digs across \nthe Great Lakes through Tuesday morning. Stronger caa behind the \nwave will drive 850mb temperatures down to around -7C to -10C, \ndropping overnight lows into the low 20s by early Tuesday morning. \nThe advent of cold air with this incoming wave will also support \nsome increasing lake effect precipitation chances as early as \ntomorrow afternoon, but more likely tomorrow evening into early \nTuesday morning. Freezing levels initially ranging between \n750ft-1500ft which may support a rain/snow mix with an afternoon \nonset. Continued cooling will pivot p-type chances to snow showers \nthrough Tuesday morning. Better coverage will be most favorable \nacross the Tri-Cities, Flint, and potentially the Thumb under \nnorthwest flow, although precipitation chance will be possible \nacross all of the cwa. \n\nHigh pressure builds in Tuesday and Wednesday, bringing an end to \nany lingering lake effect activity while supporting the return of \ndry weather. Some moderation of temperatures to follow Wednesday as \nthe thermal trough pivots east. \n\nAttention then turns to an increasingly favorable period for \nprecipitation Thursday into Friday as another mid-level disturbance \ndrops southeast from western Canada into the northern and central \nPlains. While forecast confidence continues to increase regarding \nprecipitation reaching SE MI, considerable uncertainty remains in \nthe wave phase evolution and its potential interaction with a \nsouthern stream wave coming in from the southern Plains. EPS \nensemble sensitivity analysis highlights a pronounced meridionally \noriented dipole in 500 mb height variability between the northern \nPlains and central CONUS with leading EOF accounting for 35% of \nensemble variance. This re-inforces considerable uncertainty in the \namplitude and placement of the northern-stream trough with potential \nimplications for its interaction with the southern-stream wave. \nRegardless, ensemble guidance continues to favor SE MI receiving \nprecipitation, but the aforementioned uncertainty carries importance \ngiven the strength/placement of forcing, resultant QPF amounts, and \nthermal profiles that dictate precipitation type. At present, NBM \nhighlights temperatures in the 30s to low 40s favoring precipitation \nof rain/snow or rain, which is reasonable given latest data and \nuncertainty consideration.",
